  "raw_text": "Step 1: Identify the radical conjugate of \\(5 + \\sqrt{3}\\). The radical conjugate is obtained by changing the sign of the square root term, which gives us \\(5 - \\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nStep 2: Compute the product of \\(5 + \\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(5 - \\sqrt{3}\\).\n\\[\n(5 + \\sqrt{3})(5 - \\sqrt{3})\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Use the difference of squares formula, which states that \\((a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\\). Here, \\(a = 5\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{3}\\).\n\\[\n(5 + \\sqrt{3})(5 - \\sqrt{3}) = 5^2 - (\\sqrt{3})^2\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Calculate the squares.\n\\[\n5^2 = 25 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (\\sqrt{3})^2 = 3\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Subtract the second square from the first.\n\\[\n25 - 3 = 22\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{22}<|im_end|>",
